Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Bluetooth: btintel_pcie: refresh state after missed alive interrupts

The doorbell retry backport checks boot_stage_cache after a timeout, but
that cache is normally refreshed by the interrupt handler. If the alive
interrupt was lost, the fallback can therefore test the old D0 state and
abort suspend with -EBUSY even though the controller reached D3.

Read the controller boot-stage register before the fallback state check.
Also clear gp0_received once per transition, so retries do not discard a
late acknowledgment of the same target state. Use long for the result
of wait_event_timeout(). Keep the existing hibernation reset behavior.

Based on Vladimir V. Kondratyev's stale-cache fix and the retry flag
handling in Ravindra's September 2 PM-flow proposal. This is a local
backport candidate, not an upstream submission or hardware-tested fix.

Link: https://lkml.iu.edu/2609.0/07887.html
Link: https://lore.kernel.org/linux-bluetooth/20260902042840.2432862-1-ravindra@intel.com/
---
diff --git a/drivers/bluetooth/btintel_pcie.c b/drivers/bluetooth/btintel_pcie.c
--- a/drivers/bluetooth/btintel_pcie.c
+++ b/drivers/bluetooth/btintel_pcie.c
@@ -2513,12 +2513,14 @@
 
 static int btintel_pcie_set_dxstate(struct btintel_pcie_data *data, u32 dxstate)
 {
-	int retry = 0, status;
+	int retry = 0;
+	long status;
 	u32 dx_intr_timeout_ms = 200;
 
+	/* A late interrupt from an earlier retry confirms the same state. */
+	data->gp0_received = false;
+
 	do {
-		data->gp0_received = false;
-
 		btintel_pcie_wr_sleep_cntrl(data, dxstate);
 
 		status = wait_event_timeout(data->gp0_wait_q, data->gp0_received,
@@ -2540,6 +2542,8 @@
 		 * Check if the controller reached the expected state and retry
 		 * the operation only if it hasn't.
 		 */
+		data->boot_stage_cache = btintel_pcie_rd_reg32(data,
+					BTINTEL_PCIE_CSR_BOOT_STAGE_REG);
 		if (dxstate == BTINTEL_PCIE_STATE_D0) {
 			if (btintel_pcie_in_d0(data))
 				return 0;
